{
  "title": "Quadriceps Contusion",
  "url": "https://www.orthobullets.com/knee-and-sports/3103/quadriceps-contusion",
  "subtitle": "Updated: Jun 1 2021",
  "sections": [
    {
      "text": "summary",
      "table": null,
      "images": [],
      "importance": "3",
      "children": [
        {
          "text": "A quadriceps contusion is a muscle injury to the quadriceps caused by a direct blow to the anterior thigh.",
          "table": null,
          "images": [],
          "importance": "3",
          "children": []
        },
        {
          "text": "Diagnosis is made clinically with tenderness and ecchymosis over the anterior thigh.",
          "table": null,
          "images": [],
          "importance": "3",
          "children": []
        },
        {
          "text": "Treatment is conservative with NSAIDs, rest and immobilization in 120 degrees of flexion using an ace wrap or hinged knee brace immediately after injury for 24-48 hours.",
          "table": null,
          "images": [],
          "importance": "-1",
          "children": []
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "Epidemiology",
      "table": null,
      "images": [],
      "importance": "3",
      "children": [
        {
          "text": "Demographics",
          "table": null,
          "images": [],
          "importance": "3",
          "children": [
            {
              "text": "2:1 male: female ratio",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "3",
              "children": []
            },
            {
              "text": "athletes",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "3",
              "children": [
                {
                  "text": "football, soccer, rugby most common sports",
                  "table": null,
                  "images": [],
                  "importance": "3",
                  "children": []
                },
                {
                  "text": "more common during competition than practice",
                  "table": null,
                  "images": [],
                  "importance": "3",
                  "children": []
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "Etiology",
      "table": null,
      "images": [],
      "importance": "-1",
      "children": [
        {
          "text": "Pathophysiology",
          "table": null,
          "images": [],
          "importance": "3",
          "children": [
            {
              "text": "mechanism of injury",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "3",
              "children": [
                {
                  "text": "a **direct blow** to thigh compressing the anterior thigh musculature into the femur",
                  "table": null,
                  "images": [],
                  "importance": "4",
                  "children": []
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "text": "pathophysiology",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "3",
              "children": [
                {
                  "text": "small muscle fiber tears lead to hemorrhage and swelling into the anterior compartment",
                  "table": null,
                  "images": [],
                  "importance": "3",
                  "children": []
                },
                {
                  "text": "myonecrosis and hematoma forms followed by scar formation then muscle regeneration",
                  "table": null,
                  "images": [],
                  "importance": "3",
                  "children": []
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
